Read ALL four v5 verdicts, the FULL v6 design and its diff from v5, the oracle README and evidence files, the oracle source/test, and check claims against actual Java and current Go. v6 answers v5: inline mode MEASURED (the target skips the hard cap and stays healthy; an unsplittable cluster fails every later inline insert; per-kind failures) so Go keeps Java's no-cap rule in inline mode with Java's operation order; capability refusal moved to the consuming TASK KIND (checked before task removal, inline task, bounce dependency) with parity for throwing kinds and declared refusal for livelock kinds, and a stated outcome for merges, inline inserts and inline deletes; insert refusal limited to immutable knobs under which no split can complete plus insertMaxCandidateClusters<1; deleteConcurrency checked at Java's consumer; poisoning limited to mutation entry points, read refusals non-poisoning, continuation replay never refused, explicit poison key outside pendingWriteCommitCheckPrefix; foreign-lease wait in heartbeat-renewing slices with a cap; progress baseline = stored count from a committed snapshot, drain loop bounded and stopping on relief; split throw site now step 1 outlier-excluded refit (MEASURED usable where Java throws; a size-penalised KMeans MEASURED ineffective at every lambda) then step 2 terminal reconcile recomputing n; empty-cluster claim qualified for a zero merge threshold; join error choice declared; hypothesis list completed. Challenge sufficiency and interactions with the rest of the design, not only whether the requested words appear. Distinguish measured target facts from remaining source hypotheses.
Preserved boundaries: GuardiANN executes in merger-supplied transactions (no invented child runner); SPFresh independent algorithms/LIRE; approved raw/nullable-array/scalar/sort extensions; C++ 7.3.77; format max 15/default 14; Lucene is separate upgrade-wide work, not Go TEXT; WS-D cannot complete with a refusal or fake backend.
